# Phase 6 - single Mac polished operation

Built 2026-08-22 on the M3 in two steps, both verified against the running daemon
and the running Hammerspoon the same evening: the menu bar first, then the
windows, model selection and language selection. The first half of this report is
the menu bar; "Second step" below is the rest.

## The smallest goal

A convenient way to see and change what the assistant is doing, without a
terminal and without a window that could appear in a screen share.

Chosen deliberately narrow: a menu bar item in Hammerspoon. Hammerspoon is
already installed, already owns the hotkeys and already holds the Accessibility
grant, so this adds no application, no bundle, no permission and no dependency.
The alternative considered was a permission free Swift bundle whose windows set
`NSWindow.sharingType = .none`, which is the only way to make a *window*
genuinely invisible to ScreenCaptureKit. That is the right answer for the
transcript and answer windows and is deliberately left for later: this step adds
no window at all, so it does not need it.

Not built in the first step, and all built in the second: the transcript window,
the answer window, model selection and recognition language selection. Still not
built at all: comprehension profiles, because Phase 5 does not exist yet.

## What was added

`STATUS_FIELDS`, one new dispatcher command. The menu bar reads the session state
several times a minute and cannot read the prose of `STATUS`: that wording is
written for a person, and improving it would silently break the indicator. Both
commands render one description of the session (`Assistant._status_fields`), so
they cannot disagree:

    OK capture=none continuous=off meeting=2026-08-22_22.13 model=meeting-assistant
       retention=ephemeral segments=0 streams=system vision_model=meeting-vision

A value containing a space would turn one field into two for the reader, and the
model identifiers come from the environment, so `require_space_free_field_values`
refuses to report one rather than emitting something that parses wrongly.

The Hammerspoon side was split, because one file could no longer be both the
configuration and the mechanism:

* `rvw_config.lua`  what the keys and the menu do; the only file to edit.
* `rvw_state.lua`   pure: parse a status reply, decide the title, decide whether
                    anything may be drawn. No hs API at all, which is what makes
                    it testable.
* `rvw_client.lua`  one command, or a sequence, through `bin/rvwctl`.
* `rvw_presenting.lua` whether this screen is being shown to other people.
* `rvw_actions.lua` run one configured entry and report it, for hotkeys and menu
                    items alike, so the two cannot drift apart.
* `rvw_menu.lua`    the menu bar item; the menu is built when it is opened.
* `rvw_hotkeys.lua` the entry point required from `~/.hammerspoon/init.lua`,
                    unchanged in name so the existing installation still works.

The title is the recording indicator, ASCII and small: `rvw -` idle, `rvw L`
listening, `R` keeping the transcript, `A` analysing continuously, `P`
presenting, and `rvw x` for no assistant at all, which must not look like an idle
one.

## Screen sharing

The rule from Phase 2 was that a screenshot must be silent. Phase 6 generalises
it: presenting mode (alt-cmd-P, or the menu) suppresses every alert, failures
included. While the screen is shared, an alert explaining why a screenshot failed
is worse than the failure, and it is the audience that reads it first. Turning
presenting *on* is therefore itself silent; turning it off is not.

Recognising a share automatically is best effort and always will be: a Meet or
Teams share inside a browser tab is indistinguishable from the tab. Zoom does
open a window of its own, and `rvw_config.sharing_windows` lists the titles that
mean it is sharing. Detection can only add certainty, never remove it: a manual
"I am presenting" is never overridden by a failure to find a sharing window, and
the menu item is disabled while a share is detected so that it cannot be turned
off by accident.

No window is created by any of this, which is the substantive answer to the
warning in the phase description. There is nothing that can appear in shared
material because there is nothing to appear.

## Tests

`test/test_menu_bar.py`, 20 tests, and `StatusFieldsTest` in
`test/test_assistant.py`, 7 more. Two kinds:

1. Static, always run: every command named in any lua file is a command the
   dispatcher registers (a menu item naming a renamed command would otherwise
   fail only when pressed, mid-conversation); every hotkey names a command or a
   local action and describes itself; the lua is plain ASCII.
2. Behavioural, through Hammerspoon's own lua: there is no standalone lua on
   these machines, so `test/lua_testing.py` evaluates the pure modules with
   `hs -c` and skips if Hammerspoon is not running. This covers the title, the
   status parsing, the whole menu built for a live session, an idle one and no
   session at all, and the alert policy.

Everything that actually calls hs (the alerts, the bindings, the menu bar item)
is left to manual verification.

