# Second step: the windows, the model and the language

## The windows, and why they are a separate program

`bin/rvw_view` (`helper/rvw_view.swift`), one process per window, started and
stopped by the menu bar or by alt-cmd-W and ctrl-alt-cmd-W. It shows either the
rolling transcript or the current answer, polled from the daemon (`TRANSCRIPT`,
`ANSWER`) every 2.0s and 0.8s.

It is Swift, and a separate program, for exactly one reason: a window that must
never appear in shared material has to be excluded by the window server itself,
and the only thing that does that is `NSWindow.sharingType = .none`. A window set
that way is invisible to ScreenCaptureKit, to `CGWindowListCreateImage`, to Zoom,
Meet and Teams, and to this assistant's own screenshot helper. Hammerspoon cannot
set it on a canvas, and it could not be added to `bin/rvw.app` either, because
re-signing that bundle voids every permission granted to it. So it is its own
binary, and it asks macOS for nothing at all: it draws text and reads a socket.

Two further rules follow from the same worry:

* If the window server does not accept the setting, the viewer dies instead of
  showing the window. A window that could be captured is worse than no window.
* A window does not outlive the session it is showing. The viewer exits after
  eight polls with no assistant answering (measured: 6.4s for the answer window),
  so a finished conversation cannot sit on the screen looking live, and reloading
  Hammerspoon closes both windows because they are its child tasks.

Measured on the M3 on 2026-08-22, which is the verification that matters here:
with the transcript window open, Hammerspoon's Accessibility view of it said
`rvw transcript true` -- the window was on screen -- while `bin/screen_capture
--target display` produced a full 3440x1440 capture of that same screen with no
trace of the window in it. The viewer had logged `OK rvw transcript is excluded
from screen capture (sharingType none)`.

## Model selection

`MODELS` reports what the endpoint serves and which of them answers, as key=value
pairs (the menu parses it with the same parser as `STATUS_FIELDS`); `SET_MODEL
<identifier>` switches. It refuses an identifier the endpoint is not serving,
because this LM Studio build answers for an unknown identifier with whatever is
loaded: accepting one would point the assistant at a model that never answers as
itself. Loading a different model is still Phase 8 work; choosing between the
loaded ones is not.

This was worth having immediately. On the M3 the endpoint turned out to be serving
`qwen3.6-35b-a3b` and an embedding model, and nothing under the `meeting-assistant`
identifier the assistant asks for, which the menu now shows at a glance.

## Language selection

`SET_LANGUAGE <code>` changes what the recogniser is told to expect, from the next
utterance onwards; already queued audio was spoken in the old language and
re-recognising it would cost more than the mistake is worth. The offered set is
`config.recognition_languages` (de, en, fr), reported in the status as `languages`
so the menu builds its list from the daemon rather than keeping its own copy, and
a code nobody offers is refused: Whisper would accept almost anything and
recognise badly, which reads as a broken microphone rather than a wrong setting.

This is language selection, not Phase 5. There are no comprehension profiles yet.

## Two things the windows forced

* `bin/rvwctl` read one 4 kB `recv`, which was enough while every reply was a
  single line. A transcript is tens of kilobytes and half a transcript in a window
  looks exactly like a transcript, so it now reads until the assistant closes the
  connection. `RVW_CONTROL_SOCKET` moves the socket for both ends, which is what
  lets a test drive the real client.
* Answers only ever existed as terminal output. `src/rvw/answer_buffer.py` keeps
  the current one -- heading, text and whether it is in progress, complete or
  failed -- fed by the same `_write_token` that writes to the terminal. A failed
  answer says so, because an answer that stopped must not sit in a window looking
  as though more were coming.

## Tests after the second step

272 in total, all passing. New: `test_answer_buffer.py` (6),
`test_control_client.py` (5, driving the real `bin/rvwctl` against a stand in
assistant), `test_view_window.py` (9: the capture exclusion and its refusal
asserted in the source, the viewer's imports, and the real binary polling a stand
in assistant and exiting when it goes away), plus the transcript, answer, model
and language commands in `test_assistant.py` and the new menus in
`test_menu_bar.py`.

`test/lua_testing.py` now forgets every loaded `rvw_*` module before loading the
one under test: the running Hammerspoon holds its own copy from whenever its
configuration was loaded, and a test that measured that copy would pass while the
checkout was broken.

## Verified on the M3, 2026-08-22 (second step)

* `util/run_tests.sh`: 272 tests, all pass.
* The transcript window opened from Hammerspoon was invisible in a full display
  capture while the Accessibility API confirmed it was on screen.
* `SET_MODEL qwen3.6-35b-a3b`, `SET_LANGUAGE de` and a refused `SET_LANGUAGE kl`
  all behaved, and `STATUS_FIELDS` reported both changes.
* The answer window exited by itself 8 polls after `QUIT`, leaving no process.

## Still rough

* Everything polls: the menu bar every five seconds, the windows every one or two.
  Cheap, but it is polling, and each poll is a system python start.
* The windows show text only, and the answer window shows the current answer, not
  a history: the session log is the history.
* Comprehension profiles are absent because Phase 5 has not been built.
* Automatic share detection covers Zoom's own share window and nothing else; the
  windows do not depend on it, but the alerts do.
* Nothing yet reports in the window when the transcript is not being retained, so
  a transcript window can show speech that is not being kept. That is the intended
  behaviour but it is not visible in the window itself.
